# Auteur Education — Security Rules

## 1. Secrets

Never:

* hardcode API keys;
* commit secrets;
* expose secrets in frontend code;
* expose secrets through `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`;
* return secrets through APIs;
* log secrets.

Server-side credentials remain server-side.

---

## 2. AI Providers

API keys for AI providers must never be exposed to the browser.

Frontend code must call the Auteur backend rather than external AI providers directly when credentials are required.

---

## 3. External Services

Keep provider credentials and sensitive integration details server-side.

Do not expose:

* provider API keys;
* database credentials;
* Stripe secret keys;
* internal service credentials.

---

## 4. Environment Variables

Environment variables containing secrets must remain server-side.

Public frontend environment variables must contain only values explicitly safe for browser exposure.

---

## 5. Validation

Treat all external input as untrusted.

Validate:

* HTTP request bodies;
* query parameters;
* path parameters;
* external provider responses;
* user-controlled content.

Do not assume AI-generated output is valid.

---

## 6. AI Output

AI-generated output must be validated before being treated as structured application data.

Never execute model output as code.

Never trust model-generated URLs, identifiers, or commands without validation.

---

## 7. Error Exposure

Never expose:

* stack traces;
* internal filesystem paths;
* credentials;
* tokens;
* database details;
* provider secrets.

Return safe user-facing errors.

---

## 8. Authorization

Hiding UI elements is not authorization.

When authentication and permissions are implemented, enforce them server-side.

Do not implement authentication prematurely merely because the final product will require it.

---

## 9. Logging

Do not log:

* passwords;
* API keys;
* authentication tokens;
* secrets;
* unnecessary sensitive user data.

Logs should contain only information useful for diagnosis.

---

## 10. Dependencies

Do not add security-sensitive dependencies without justification.

Keep dependencies minimal.

Prefer maintained, established packages when an external dependency is genuinely required.

---

## 11. Demo vs Production

Temporary demo configurations must not be mistaken for production security architecture.

Explicitly identify temporary:

* CORS;
* authentication bypasses;
* mock credentials;
* in-memory state;
* development-only behavior.

Never commit real credentials to support a demo.

---

## 12. Security Principle

Security should be enforced at the actual trust boundary.

Frontend behavior improves UX.

Backend behavior enforces security.

External providers must be treated as untrusted dependencies.

User input and AI output must both be validated.
